1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,070
Energy gets wasted for a boring reason:

2
00:00:03,210 --> 00:00:06,900
nobody trusts the forecast enough to act on it.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:10,046
Last year I built Agentic Arbiter, on my own.

4
00:00:10,186 --> 00:00:12,400
It's live, and this is it running.

5
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:14,805
Data centres run chillers even when

6
00:00:14,945 --> 00:00:17,400
the air outside is already cold enough.

7
00:00:17,700 --> 00:00:20,550
I pick a real site, and the plant's own limits.

8
00:00:20,690 --> 00:00:22,400
Then I let the agent decide.

9
00:00:22,700 --> 00:00:26,900
It works hour by hour, deciding when it's safe to shut the chillers off.

10
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,230
It made no unsafe calls.

11
00:00:30,370 --> 00:00:33,400
The old controller made one.

12
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:37,900
Over five real years, that's about four hundred extra hours a year.

13
00:00:38,200 --> 00:00:39,552
And it grades itself.

14
00:00:39,692 --> 00:00:42,733
Here it's missing its own target, and saying so.

15
00:00:42,873 --> 00:00:44,900
That's the part I'm proudest of.

16
00:00:45,200 --> 00:00:46,980
Second of eight hundred and forty-two teams.

17
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:48,900
The UN's Cool Coalition picked it up.

18
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:51,558
Pakistan has solar on millions of roofs,

19
00:00:51,698 --> 00:00:53,718
but the grid can't see it.

20
00:00:53,858 --> 00:00:57,900
Go over two hundred units a month, and every unit gets re-priced.

21
00:00:58,200 --> 00:01:00,387
So I'm building a Solar Home Agent.

22
00:01:00,527 --> 00:01:04,900
It reads tomorrow's weather, then picks when to run the motor and the iron.

23
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:09,900
Same habit: it remembers how wrong it's been.

24
00:01:10,200 --> 00:01:13,320
Moving a few jobs into the afternoon keeps this home under the limit.

25
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:14,900
About five thousand rupees this month.

26
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:17,078
Nearly nineteen thousand over the next six.

27
00:01:17,218 --> 00:01:19,900
It's a simulation, on one real month of Islamabad weather.

28
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:24,056
When it isn't sure, it says no,

29
00:01:24,196 --> 00:01:26,400
and tells you why.

30
00:01:26,700 --> 00:01:30,200
It keeps score against what the sun actually did.

31
00:01:30,500 --> 00:01:34,200
But look what it can't fix.

32
00:01:34,500 --> 00:01:37,188
It still wastes most of its midday sun, and buys after dark.

33
00:01:37,328 --> 00:01:38,000
Storage closes that.

34
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:40,869
Next: five to ten homes in Islamabad, over WhatsApp.

35
00:01:41,009 --> 00:01:44,435
I'm starting an Energy-and-AI circle at COMSATS, and I'd join IRENA NewGen.

36
00:01:44,575 --> 00:01:48,000
Then add those homes into one neighbourhood forecast, with our distribution company.

37
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:50,462
The solar is already on Pakistan's roofs.

38
00:01:50,602 --> 00:01:54,000
I want to help the grid see it, and trust it.

